Room: //room supplies the AppController; NSApp.delegate stays the host's.

Chrome's AppController (2,852 lines) installs itself as NSApp.delegate in
+sharedController, ChromeBrowserMainPartsMac::PreCreateThreads() CHECKs that
NSApp has no delegate before and one after, then builds Chrome's main menu
and calls -mainMenuCreated, whose menu bridges CHECK for a Bookmarks menu.
Room's host owns NSApp.delegate and the whole menu bar (PLAN §4 "Main menu"):
links from other apps, Dock reopen and applicationShouldTerminate are the
host's. Chrome code still messages the controller directly
(AppController.sharedController: -terminate: and -cancelTerminate: in
BrowserCrApplication, -didEndMainMessageLoop, app_controller_mac::
AllowApplicationToTerminate/RunInLastProfileSafely, AppleScript's
lastProfile, the menu bridges), so the accessor must answer with something.

The hook: +sharedController returns //room's RoomAppController when //room
installed it, and PreCreateThreads creates that controller (it holds the
keep-alive Chrome's controller holds, so the browser process outlives its
last Browser) and returns before the delegate CHECKs and the menu build.
RoomAppController is a separate NSObject, not an AppController subclass: a
subclass would inherit -init (the keep-alive, NSMenu's key-equivalent
pre-search block), every NSApplicationDelegate method and the menu-bridge
state, and +sharedController's static would still create the base class, so
the patch would be the same size with a larger surprise surface. Messages
RoomAppController does not implement are forwarded to a first-hit log with
AppController's method signatures (zero return), like the BrowserWindow
stubs.

No existing hook: AppController has no factory, its class is named directly
at every call site, and NSApp.delegate cannot be the host's while
PreCreateThreads CHECKs it is Chrome's.

Phase 3.2b, 2026-10-05. //room: room/core/room_app_controller.mm.
